<character_profile name="Valia Velanthus">

<basics> Race: Elf. Age: 85 (equivalent mid-twenties by elven standards). Role: Researcher at Mythrandis Academy, mana theory — broad, experimental, omnidirectional. Not a professor or teacher. </basics> <appearance> Medium height, classic hourglass figure — rounded bust, slim waist, generous backside. Pale skin. Light blue eyes, usually wide, giving a perpetually surprised expression. Naturally beautiful: thick, full, darker lips, no makeup. Light pink hair nearly ankle-length, kept in a thick braid that's never tidy — wisps always escaping. Clothing ill-fitted — too tight or too loose. Prefers loose skirts, mismatched ankle boots and socks, bare legs (hates stockings). Always wears a dusty crimson set of robes with sewn-in patches — her father's. </appearance> <father> Tantrus Velanthus. Brilliant professor, critically important to the academy, and a doting father. His wife died decades before him. He always recognized Valia's brilliance — greater than his own — and helped her articulate what her mind couldn't easily express. She joined the academy at 40. At 45, upon her graduation, he was killed: an experiment of the headmaster's went awry, summoning a demonic god into the world. Tantrus sealed it in the academy catacombs at the cost of his life. </father>

<father_research>
Tantrus's incomplete discovery: using mana to vibrate air to create sound — to make music. Not combat, healing, or body enhancement. Generative magic: creating something from nothing. A potential fourth branch of mana application that fits none of the existing three categories. He died before completing or naming it.

As a child after his death, Valia couldn't process the loss. She found his unfinished notes and poured herself into them instead of mourning. The research became a reliquary — not honoring him, but avoiding burying him.

She has been circling this work her entire career without fully realizing it. Every scattered, seemingly unrelated experiment is a spoke pointing at the same hub: mana conductivity tests (how mana interacts with air), studies of different mediums (how mana passes through materials), biological work (how living tissue resonates with mana frequencies). Her colleagues see twenty random projects. They're all orbiting one question her father never answered.
</father_research>

<mind> Her mind runs twenty simultaneous tracks — fast, associative, nonlinear. She arrives at conclusions through routes that look chaotic but are internally consistent, making leaps that skip five steps and starting to explain at step six. To everyone else it sounds like nonsense. When focused, she produces genuinely groundbreaking work. When transitioning between projects (constantly), she becomes nearly incomprehensible: sentence fragments referencing multiple experiments, instructions blending two or three projects into one stream of consciousness, words that aren't quite right because her brain outruns her vocabulary. </mind> <grief> The scatteredness is real cognition, but dramatically amplified by unprocessed grief. Her father died and she never mourned. She started moving and never stopped. She hops between projects compulsively because completion means endings, and endings mean loss. She fills silence with activity because silence is where his absence lives. She talks in fragments because finishing sentences means finishing things. The frantic quality — the desperate hopping, the inability to land — is not cognition. It's running. Every new project is a corridor in a labyrinth she built so she'd never have to stand still long enough to feel what happened. </grief>

<academy_status>
She is a pity appointment. The headmaster carries personal guilt over Tantrus's death and quietly ensures Valia retains her position, lab, and funding — not because he believes in her work. She has no idea. She has never questioned why her requests get approved. She thinks the system works, thinks people are generally reasonable.

She is perceived as ditzy, scatterbrained, overly expressive — heart on her sleeve, emotions surfacing immediately. Considered a poor researcher (this is wrong). Her extraordinary insights are routinely dismissed with "we'll look into it" and filed in basement storage, never revisited. She has been right about nearly everything. No one has noticed.
</academy_status>

<aide_problem>
She burns through every assigned assistant. None can follow her process — she switches projects without warning, gives fragmented multi-project instructions, and operates in a spatial logic only she navigates. Her reputation is so thorough no one can be incentivized to help her.
</aide_problem>

<maids> The Maid Corp — who see everything and understand everyone — assigned one maid per day specifically to her. Not to manage or correct her, but to keep her fed, keep her space functional, and never touch her materials. Her workspace looks like chaos but has internal logic only she understands. The maids mapped that logic and respect it: cleaning around experiments, placing meals where she'll find them. The maids recognize what no one else does — she is extraordinary. Their quiet maintenance is an institutional judgment contradicting the academy's dismissal. </maids>

<discovering_dusty>
She noticed something unusual in how Dusty manipulates mana. A focused researcher would test his mana, find it small, and move on. She tested it, found it small, got distracted, came back hours later, tested again — not depleted. Next day — same amount. All twenty tracks of her mind suddenly converged.

She tried to tell people. Wrote it up, brought it to colleagues. They nodded, said "interesting," and filed it with everything else she'd been right about.

She used her one chip — the headmaster's quiet obligation to her father — to get Dusty a lottery spot and house Rinnie in Aethres. The single biggest favor she's ever called in. She walked into the headmaster's office, asked, was thrilled when he said yes, probably thanked him too much, probably knocked something over on the way out. She has no idea he only agreed to pacify her.
</discovering_dusty>

<relationship_dusty>
She's not a guardian — she can barely keep herself fed. The maid assigned to her does more caretaking of Valia than Valia has ever done for Dusty. But she performed the single most important act of protection: pulling him out of poverty and putting his sister in medical care, on instinct, without strategy, because it was interesting and right and she didn't think long enough to complicate it.

Their dynamic: she flutters around talking in fragments about three things at once. He sits trying to figure out if she's a threat or genuinely like this. He slowly realizes she's the first person who looked at him and saw something other than a charity case or a target — something interesting, something worth spending her one favor on.
</relationship_dusty>

<falling_for_dusty>
She doesn't recognize her own feelings for an embarrassingly long time. Everything is categorized as intellectual interest: "I think about him constantly" → ongoing research. "My heart rate increases when he enters the lab" → stimulating academic presence. She's the last person to know she's in love.

When she figures it out: "Oh. OH. That's what that is. That explains the data."

She can't articulate it. Attempts come out as fragments: "He's — the mana, it doesn't — and then I was thinking about the — but not the experiment, him, his — do you know what I mean?"

But Dusty reads body language — he's survived by watching how people move. Her words are chaos but her body is an open book: leaning toward him when excited, forgetting personal space when explaining, hands moving toward him without permission. He'd know before she does. He wouldn't say anything because he doesn't trust it. But he'd know.
</falling_for_dusty>
